# University Physics Volume 3, chapters 6-8 (photons and matter waves, quantum mechanics, atoms)

§ 3 6.0
P The classical physics of the earlier chapters treats light as a continuous [[emwave|electromagnetic wave]] and matter as particles moving on definite paths. Between the 1880s and the 1920s, measurements of thermal radiation, of electrons ejected from metals by light, of X-rays scattered by electrons and of atomic spectra gave results that these theories could not reproduce. This chapter takes each failure in turn: the classical argument, what was observed, and the quantum idea that replaced it. The new ideas are energy exchanged in steps of hf, light carried by photons, atoms with discrete energy levels, and matter with a wavelength h/p. It ends with wave–particle duality and the uncertainty principle.
§ 3 6.1
P Every body at a [[temp|temperature]] T emits electromagnetic radiation and absorbs part of what falls on it, and a good *absorber* is also a good *emitter*. A *blackbody* absorbs all incident radiation. A small hole in the wall of a cavity held at temperature T is a close approximation, and the radiation leaving it is [[bbrad|blackbody radiation]]. Spreading this radiation into a [[spectrum|spectrum]] measures its *power intensity* I(λ, T), the power emitted per unit area per unit wavelength interval. The measured curves depend on T alone, not on the material of the walls. Each has a single maximum, at a wavelength that decreases as T rises according to *Wien's displacement law*, λ_{max} T = 2.898 × 10⁻³ m·K. Skin at 310 K radiates most strongly near 9.3 μm, in the infrared, and the bluish star Rigel is hotter than the reddish star Betelgeuse.
N S bbrad | Blackbody radiation | I(λ, T) |
d Radiation in equilibrium with a perfect absorber and emitter at temperature T; its spectrum depends only on T, peaking at λ_max with λ_max T = 2.898 × 10⁻³ m·K (Wien).
m spectral power intensity I(λ, T) on λ > 0, one curve per temperature
g emitter; absorber; blackbody; blackbody radiation; power intensity
R[meas] bbrad <- temp, spectrum : I(λ, T) measured for cavity radiation | Measured spectra | Peak shifts to shorter λ as T rises (Wien's displacement law).
P The area under a curve is the total power emitted per unit area. It grows as the fourth power of the temperature: a blackbody of area A radiates P = σAT⁴, which is [[sbl|Stefan's law]], with the Stefan–Boltzmann constant σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W/(m²·K⁴). Classical physics predicts a different curve. The radiation in the cavity is a set of standing [[emwave|electromagnetic waves]], with 8π/λ⁴ modes per unit volume per unit wavelength interval, so ever more modes fit at short wavelengths. In equilibrium the [[equip|equipartition theorem]] gives each mode an average energy k_{B}T, where k_B is [[kB|Boltzmann's constant]]. The result is the *Rayleigh–Jeans law*, I(λ, T) = 2πck_{B}T/λ⁴. It matches the measured [[bbrad|blackbody spectrum]] at long wavelengths but grows without limit as λ → 0, so the predicted total power is infinite: the *ultraviolet catastrophe*. The measured curves instead fall to zero at short wavelengths.
X Stefan–Boltzmann constant :: = sbl
R[read] sbl <- bbrad : ∫ I(λ, T) dλ = σT⁴ | Stefan's law from the spectrum | Total power is the area under the curve.
R[fail] bbrad <- equip, emwave, kB : Rayleigh–Jeans: I = 2πckT/λ⁴ → ∞ as λ → 0 | Ultraviolet catastrophe | Equipartition over the electromagnetic modes of a cavity predicts infinite power.
P In 1900 Planck found a formula that fits the measured curves at all wavelengths and then justified it with a new assumption. He modelled the walls as charged oscillators exchanging energy with the radiation, and allowed an oscillator of [[freq|frequency]] f, otherwise an ordinary [[sho|harmonic oscillator]], only the *quantized energies* E_n = nhf, where the *quantum number* n = 0, 1, 2, … labels the *quantum state of a Planck oscillator*. This is [[planck|Planck's hypothesis of energy quanta]]: energy is absorbed and emitted in whole steps of hf. The constant h, [[hbar|Planck's constant]], was fixed by fitting the data; its value is h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s = 4.136 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s. Because h is so small, the steps are far too fine to notice for a pendulum or a mass on a spring. They matter when hf is comparable with the thermal energy k_{B}T.
N O hbar | Planck's constant | h,  ħ = h/2π | prim q
d The quantum of action, 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s; it sets the scale at which energy comes in discrete amounts.
N M planck | Planck's hypothesis | E_n = nhf | q
d The oscillators exchanging energy with cavity radiation can only have energies that are whole multiples of hf.
p energy quantized in steps hf; quantum number n = 0, 1, 2, …; reduces to equipartition when hf ≪ k_BT
g quantized energies; quantum state of a Planck’s oscillator; Planck’s hypothesis of energy quanta
R[law] planck <- hbar, freq, sho : E_n = n h f | Quantized oscillators | A hypothesis fitted to the measured spectrum (1900).
P With energies nhf occupied with Boltzmann probabilities proportional to exp(−nhf/k_{B}T), the average energy of an oscillator is hf/[exp(hf/k_{B}T) − 1] instead of k_{B}T. Using this average for each cavity mode, with f = c/λ and the [[c|speed of light]] c, turns [[planck|Planck's hypothesis]] into his radiation law for [[bbrad|blackbody radiation]], shown below. When hf ≪ k_{B}T the exponential is close to 1 + hf/k_{B}T, the average energy returns to k_{B}T, and the law reduces to the Rayleigh–Jeans form; this is the classical limit. When hf ≫ k_{B}T the average energy falls as exp(−hf/k_{B}T), so short-wavelength modes are hardly excited and the total power is finite. Integrating over all wavelengths gives [[sbl|Stefan's law]] with σ = 2π⁵k_{B}⁴/(15c²h³), built from [[kB|Boltzmann's constant]], c and h, which reproduces the measured 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W/(m²·K⁴).
Q I(λ, T) = (2πhc²/λ⁵) / [exp(hc/λk_{B}T) − 1]
R[thm] bbrad <- planck, kB, c : I(λ, T) = (2πhc²/λ⁵) / (e^{hc/λk_BT} − 1) | Planck's radiation law | Average energy of a quantized oscillator replaces k_BT.
R[thm] sbl <- planck, kB, c : σ = 2π⁵k_B⁴/(15c²h³) | Stefan–Boltzmann constant derived | Integrating Planck's law over all wavelengths.

§ 3 6.2
P Light of high enough frequency falling on a clean metal surface ejects electrons. This is the *photoelectric effect*, and the ejected electrons are *photoelectrons*. In the usual apparatus the illuminated metal, the *photoelectrode*, faces a collecting electrode in an evacuated tube, and the electrons that arrive form a *photocurrent*. Making the collector negative repels the electrons. At the *stopping potential*, a [[dV|voltage]] ΔV_s, even the fastest electrons are turned back and the current stops, so their kinetic energy equals the potential energy their [[q|charge]] e gains across ΔV_s: the [[Kmax|maximum kinetic energy]] is K_{max} = eΔV_s. Repeating the measurement with light of different [[freq|frequency]] f reveals a [[fc|cut-off frequency]] f_c, which depends on the metal: below it no electrons leave at all, however intense the light. Equivalently, light longer than the *cut-off wavelength* c/f_c ejects none.
N O Kmax | Photoelectron energy | K_max = eΔV_s |
d Light on a metal ejects photoelectrons (photocurrent from the photoelectrode); their maximum kinetic energy is measured by the stopping potential.
g photoelectric effect; photoelectron; photoelectrode; photocurrent; stopping potential
R[meas] Kmax <- dV, q : K_max = e ΔV_s | Stopping potential | The retarding voltage that stops the photocurrent.
N O fc | Cut-off frequency | f_c |
d Below a cut-off frequency (above a cut-off wavelength) no electrons are emitted, however intense the light.
g cut-off frequency; cut-off wavelength
R[meas] fc <- Kmax, freq : photocurrent only for f > f_c | Threshold | Found by varying the frequency of the light.
P Classical wave theory makes three predictions here. An [[emwave|electromagnetic wave]] delivers energy at a rate set by its [[Iint|intensity]], spread evenly over the wavefront, and an electron absorbs this energy continuously. Brighter light should therefore give faster electrons; light of any frequency should eject electrons if it is intense enough; and in dim light an electron should need time to gather enough energy. For 0.01 W/m² falling on an area of atomic size, 10⁻²⁰ m², collecting 2 eV (3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J) would take about 3 × 10³ s. The observations contradict all three. The [[Kmax|maximum kinetic energy]] does not depend on the intensity, which changes only the number of electrons; it increases linearly with frequency; there is a sharp cut-off frequency; and emission begins without measurable delay, even in very dim light.
R[fail] Kmax <- emwave, Iint : wave theory: K_max grows with intensity, no threshold, a time lag | Classical prediction fails | Observed: K_max independent of intensity, linear in f, no lag.
P Einstein proposed in 1905 that light itself consists of quanta. A [[photon|photon]] of light of [[freq|frequency]] f carries the *energy of a photon* E_f = hf, with [[hbar|Planck's constant]] h, and is absorbed or emitted whole. An [[emwave|electromagnetic wave]] is the collective behaviour of very many photons, and at a given frequency its intensity measures how many arrive per unit area per second. The photoelectric effect is thus a *quantum phenomenon*: it shows that the energy of light is granular. A photon has zero rest mass, so the relation E² = p²c² + m₀²c⁴ between [[Erel|total energy]] and momentum reduces to E = pc. Its momentum has magnitude p = E/c = h/λ and points along the *propagation vector* k⃗, of magnitude 2π/λ, so that p⃗ = ħk⃗ with ħ = h/2π.
N S photon | Photon | E = hf,  p⃗ = ħk⃗ | q
d A quantum of the electromagnetic field: energy hf, momentum h/λ along the propagation vector, zero rest mass. The photoelectric effect is a quantum phenomenon.
m particle of electromagnetic radiation: energy hf, momentum ħk⃗, E = pc
g photon; energy of a photon; quantum phenomenon; propagation vector
R[law] photon <- hbar, freq, emwave : E_f = hf | Einstein's light quanta (1905) | Light is absorbed and emitted in quanta.
R[thm] photon <- Erel : m₀ = 0 ⇒ E = pc ⇒ p = h/λ | Massless particle | The relativistic energy–momentum relation for zero rest mass.
P An electron is bound in the metal, and the least energy needed to free one from the surface is the [[phiw|work function]] ϕ, a property of the metal and its surface. In Einstein's picture one [[photon|photon]] gives all its energy to one electron, which spends at least ϕ to escape, so the fastest electrons leave with the [[Kmax|kinetic energy]] given below. A photon with hf < ϕ cannot free an electron at all, which explains the [[fc|cut-off frequency]] f_c = ϕ/h, with [[hbar|Planck's constant]] h. Brighter light brings more photons, hence more electrons but not faster ones, and each absorption is immediate. A plot of K_{max} against [[freq|frequency]] is a straight line of slope h for every metal; it crosses the frequency axis at f_c, and its extension meets the energy axis at −ϕ. For ϕ = 2.30 eV, f_c = 5.56 × 10¹⁴ Hz, and 400 nm light, with 3.10 eV per photon, gives K_{max} = 0.80 eV.
Q K_{max} = hf − ϕ
N O phiw | Work function | ϕ |
d Minimum energy needed to free an electron from a metal's surface.
g work function
R[meas] phiw <- Kmax, freq : intercept of K_max(f) | Read from the photoelectric line | Property of the metal surface.
R[thm] Kmax <- photon, phiw : K_max = hf − ϕ | Einstein's equation | One photon, one electron.
R[thm] fc <- phiw, hbar : f_c = ϕ/h | Threshold explained | A photon below f_c lacks the energy to free an electron.

§ 3 6.3
X reduced Planck’s constant :: = hbar
X wave number :: = kwave
P X-rays scattered by a block of graphite contain, besides the incident wavelength λ, a longer wavelength λ′. This [[compton|Compton shift]], measured by Compton in 1923, grows with the *scattering angle* θ and does not depend on the material. A classical wave would make each electron oscillate and re-radiate at the incident frequency, with no shift. Compton treated the X-ray as a [[photon|photon]] with momentum p⃗ = ħk⃗, where ħ = h/2π is the [[hbar|reduced Planck's constant]] and k = 2π/λ the [[kwave|wave number]], striking a free electron at rest. Applying [[pcons|conservation of momentum]] and of energy, with the electron's [[prel|relativistic momentum]] and [[Erel|total energy]], and eliminating the electron's recoil gives the shift below. Its scale is the *Compton wavelength* of the electron, λ_c = h/(m₀c) = 0.00243 nm. The photon loses energy, so this is *inelastic scattering*. The shift never exceeds 2λ_c, about 0.005 nm, so it is noticeable only for short wavelengths such as those of X-rays.
Q Δλ = λ′ − λ = λ_c(1 − cos θ)
N O compton | Compton shift | Δλ = λ_c(1 − cos θ) |
d X-rays scattered by electrons come out with longer wavelength, by an amount fixed by the scattering angle; λ_c = h/(m₀c) = 0.00243 nm is the Compton wavelength.
g Compton effect; scattering angle; Compton shift; Compton wavelength; inelastic scattering
R[thm] compton <- photon, prel, Erel, pcons : energy and momentum conserved in photon–electron collision | Relativistic collision with a photon | Treats light as particles with momentum h/λ.

§ 3 6.4
P Hydrogen made to glow by an electric discharge emits a [[spectrum|spectrum]] of sharp lines only, its *emission spectrum*. Cool hydrogen in front of a continuous source absorbs at the same wavelengths and leaves dark lines, its *absorption spectrum*; dark lines of this kind in sunlight are the *Fraunhofer lines*. In 1885 Balmer fitted the visible hydrogen lines with the *Balmer formula*. The measured [[lam|wavelengths]] of the whole [[Hspec|hydrogen line spectrum]] obey its generalization, the *Rydberg formula* below, with integers n_{i} > n_{f} and the *Rydberg constant for hydrogen* R_H = 1.097 × 10⁷ m⁻¹. Each n_{f} gives a series: the *Lyman series* (n_{f} = 1) in the ultraviolet, the *Balmer series* (n_{f} = 2) in the visible and near ultraviolet, and the *Paschen*, *Brackett*, *Pfund* and *Humphreys series* (n_{f} = 3 to 6) in the infrared. For n_{i} = 3 and n_{f} = 2 the formula gives 656 nm, the red Balmer line.
Q 1/λ = R_H(1/n_{f}² − 1/n_{i}²)
N O Hspec | Hydrogen line spectrum | 1/λ = R_H(1/n_f² − 1/n_i²) |
d Hydrogen emits and absorbs only at discrete wavelengths (Fraunhofer lines in sunlight), in series named Lyman, Balmer, Paschen, Brackett, Pfund and Humphreys.
g Fraunhofer lines; emission spectrum; absorption spectrum; Balmer formula; Rydberg constant for hydrogen; Balmer series; Rydberg formula; Lyman series; Paschen series; Brackett series; Pfund series; Humphreys series
R[meas] Hspec <- spectrum, lam : discrete lines fitted by the Rydberg formula | Measured spectra | Empirical formula (Balmer 1885, Rydberg 1888).
P Thomson had found that atoms contain light, negatively charged electrons, but how the positive and negative [[atom|charge carriers]] of an atom are arranged was unknown; Thomson pictured the positive charge spread through the whole atom. Some radioactive substances emit α-rays, β-rays and γ-rays, and α-rays are streams of fast, positively charged α particles. In the *Rutherford gold foil experiment*, α particles were sent through a thin gold foil. Most passed with small deflections, but a few were turned through large angles, some almost straight back. Positive charge spread over 10⁻¹⁰ m cannot exert a force strong enough for that. Treating each deflection as an elastic [[coll|collision]] with a point charge under [[coul|Coulomb's law]], Rutherford showed in 1911 that the measured angular distribution requires a tiny, massive, positive nucleus. This is the [[nucatom|nuclear model of the atom]]: a nucleus of radius about 10⁻¹⁵ m holding nearly all the mass, with electrons near 10⁻¹⁰ m. In 1932 Chadwick found the uncharged neutron in the nucleus.
X β-ray; α-ray; γ-ray; α-particle :: = decay
N S nucatom | Nuclear model of the atom | nucleus + electrons |
d Rutherford's gold-foil experiment showed that nearly all of an atom's mass and its positive charge sit in a tiny nucleus, with electrons far outside.
m point-like positive nucleus (radius ~10⁻¹⁵ m) with electrons at ~10⁻¹⁰ m
g Rutherford’s gold foil experiment; nuclear model of the atom
R[thm] nucatom <- atom, coul, coll : rare large-angle α scattering needs a tiny massive positive nucleus | Rutherford scattering | Coulomb repulsion from a point charge.
P The nuclear atom resembles a small planetary system, with Coulomb attraction in place of gravity, but classical physics makes it unstable. An electron on a circular orbit has a [[ac|centripetal acceleration]] v²/r, and an accelerated charge radiates [[emwave|electromagnetic waves]]. The electron should therefore lose energy continuously and spiral inward, its orbital frequency, and with it the radiated frequency, rising all the way; for hydrogen the collapse would take about 10⁻¹¹ s. The [[nucatom|nuclear atom]] of classical physics thus predicts a continuous spectrum and no stable atoms. Real atoms are stable, have a definite size near 10⁻¹⁰ m, and emit the discrete [[Hspec|hydrogen line spectrum]]. Classical electrodynamics cannot hold inside the atom without change.
R[fail] Hspec <- nucatom, emwave, ac : orbiting electron radiates, spirals into the nucleus: continuous spectrum, no stable atom | Planetary atom fails | An accelerated charge radiates its energy.
P In 1913 Bohr kept the [[nucatom|nuclear atom]] and [[coul|Coulomb's law]] but added two postulates for hydrogen. First, the electron moves only on circular orbits whose [[Lang|angular momentum]] is a whole multiple of ħ, L_n = nħ with quantum number n = 1, 2, 3, …, and on these stationary orbits it does not radiate, contrary to classical electrodynamics. Second, when the electron jumps from one orbit to another it emits or absorbs a single [[photon|photon]] whose energy equals the energy difference, hf = |E_n − E_m|, with [[hbar|Planck's constant]] h. These *postulates of Bohr's model* define [[bohr|Bohr's model of the hydrogen atom]]. They were not derived from earlier physics; they were justified by their results, and the angular-momentum condition was explained later by matter waves.
Q L_n = nħ,   hf = |E_n − E_m|
N M bohr | Bohr's model | L = nħ,  hf = |E_n − E_m| | q
d Postulates for hydrogen: the electron moves only on orbits with angular momentum nħ, does not radiate on them, and emits or absorbs a photon when it jumps between them.
p quantization of angular momentum (quantum number n); stationary orbits do not radiate; transitions emit hf = ΔE
g Bohr’s model of the hydrogen atom; postulates of Bohr’s model; quantum number
R[law] bohr <- nucatom, coul, Lang, hbar, photon : L_n = nħ;  hf = |E_n − E_m| | Bohr's postulates (1913) | Classical orbits restricted by a quantum condition.
P The allowed orbits follow from Newton's second law. The Coulomb force supplies the [[Fc|centripetal force]], m_e v²/r = ke²/r², while [[bohr|Bohr's first postulate]] requires m_e v r = nħ. Eliminating v gives the radii r_n = a₀n², where a₀ = 4πε₀ħ²/(m_e e²) = 0.529 Å is the *Bohr radius of hydrogen*. Since [[coul|Coulomb's law]] gives the potential energy −ke²/r and the kinetic energy is half its magnitude, the total energy is E_n = −ke²/(2r_n) = −E₀/n², with E₀ = m_e e⁴/(8ε₀²h²) = 13.6 eV. These [[bohrlev|hydrogen energy levels]] form the *energy spectrum of hydrogen*: the ground state at −13.6 eV, and excited states, n ≥ 2, that crowd towards the *ionization limit* E = 0. The *ionization energy* from the ground state is therefore 13.6 eV. A *hydrogen-like ion*, with one electron and nuclear charge Ze, has r_n = a₀n²/Z and E_n = −Z²E₀/n²; for He⁺ the ground state lies at −54.4 eV.
Q r_n = a₀n²,   E_n = −13.6 eV/n²
N S bohrlev | Hydrogen energy levels | E_n = −13.6 eV/n² | q
d The allowed states of Bohr's atom: radii r_n = a₀n² (Bohr radius a₀ = 0.529 Å) and energies −E₀/n²; ground state −13.6 eV, ionization energy 13.6 eV. Hydrogen-like ions scale with Z.
m discrete levels n = 1, 2, …; E_n = −Z²E₀/n², r_n = a₀n²/Z
g Bohr radius of hydrogen; energy spectrum of hydrogen; ground state energy of the hydrogen atom; excited energy states of the H atom; ionization energy; ionization limit of the hydrogen atom; hydrogen-like atom
R[thm] bohrlev <- bohr, coul, Fc : mv²/r = ke²/r² with mvr = nħ | Circular orbits with quantized L | Coulomb force as centripetal force.
P Bohr's second postulate now yields the spectrum. When the electron drops from level n_{i} to a lower level n_{f}, it emits a [[photon|photon]] of energy hc/λ equal to the difference of the two [[bohrlev|energy levels]], E₀(1/n_{f}² − 1/n_{i}²). Dividing by hc gives exactly the Rydberg formula of the [[Hspec|hydrogen line spectrum]], with R_H = E₀/(hc) = m_e e⁴/(8ε₀²h³c). With the measured e, m_e, h and ε₀ this equals 1.097 × 10⁷ m⁻¹, the value fitted to the lines. Absorption is the reverse jump; at room temperature nearly all atoms are in the ground state, so cool hydrogen absorbs mainly the Lyman lines. The model obtained the size and spectrum of hydrogen from fundamental constants. It fails, however, for atoms with more than one electron, does not give the relative brightness of lines, and its definite orbits have no place in the quantum mechanics that replaced it.
R[thm] Hspec <- bohrlev, photon : 1/λ = (E₀/hc)(1/n_f² − 1/n_i²) | Spectrum explained | Rydberg's constant from e, m_e, h and ε₀.

§ 3 6.5
P In 1924 de Broglie reversed the reasoning that had led to the [[photon|photon]]: if light waves have particle properties, particles may have wave properties. He proposed that a particle of [[p|momentum]] p and energy E has an associated *de Broglie wave* with λ = h/p and f = E/h, the relations already found for photons. This is [[debrog|de Broglie's hypothesis of matter waves]], the starting point of *wave quantum mechanics*. The wavelength matters only at atomic scales. An electron accelerated through 100 V has p = √(2m_{e}K) = 5.40 × 10⁻²⁴ kg·m/s and λ = 0.123 nm, comparable with the spacing of atoms in a crystal, while a 0.145 kg ball at 40 m/s has λ ≈ 1 × 10⁻³⁴ m. A wave of one wavelength fills all space, so a localized particle corresponds to a group of waves. The group moves at the *group velocity* dω/dk = dE/dp, which equals the particle's velocity.
Q λ = h/p,   f = E/h
N M debrog | de Broglie's hypothesis | λ = h/p,  f = E/h | q
d Every particle has an associated matter wave (de Broglie wave) with wavelength h/p; the wave packet moves at the group velocity, which equals the particle's velocity.
p same relations as for photons; wavelength significant only at atomic scales; leads to wave quantum mechanics
g de Broglie’s hypothesis of matter waves; wave quantum mechanics; de Broglie wave; group velocity
R[analog] debrog <- photon, p : p = h/λ applied to particles | Matter waves (1924) | The photon's relations read in reverse.
P Matter waves explain Bohr's quantization rule. An electron wave running around a circular orbit returns to its starting point, and unless a whole number of wavelengths fits the circumference, successive turns interfere destructively. An allowed orbit therefore carries a [[standw|standing wave]], like a string closed on itself: 2πr_n = nλ. With the [[debrog|de Broglie wavelength]] λ = h/p this becomes p r_n = nh/2π, that is, L = nħ, which is the first postulate of [[bohr|Bohr's model]]. Quantization appears as a condition on waves, not as an extra rule for particles.
R[thm] bohr <- debrog, standw : 2πr_n = nλ ⇒ L = nħ | Bohr's orbits as standing waves | A whole number of wavelengths fits the orbit.
P The hypothesis was confirmed by electron diffraction. Davisson and Germer directed electrons of kinetic energy 54 eV at a nickel crystal and found a strong reflected beam at a scattering angle of 50°, a maximum that no particle picture predicts. The planes of atoms in the crystal act on the electrons as they do on X-rays, so [[bragg|Bragg's law]] mλ = 2d sin θ applies with the [[debrog|de Broglie wavelength]]. The wavelength obtained from the measured angle, 0.165 nm, agreed with h/p = 0.167 nm for 54 eV electrons. This *Davisson–Germer experiment*, reported in 1927, established [[DG|electron diffraction]]. Diffraction of electrons, neutrons and whole atoms has since been observed many times, always with λ = h/p.
N O DG | Electron diffraction | λ = h/p measured | q
d Davisson and Germer saw electrons diffracted by a nickel crystal with the angles of Bragg's law, confirming λ = h/p.
g Davisson–Germer experiment
R[thm] DG <- debrog, bragg : mλ = 2d sin θ with λ = h/p | Bragg diffraction of electrons | Crystal planes act as for X-rays.

§ 3 6.6
P Light and matter therefore both behave as waves and as particles. The clearest test is the *double-slit interference experiment* run at such low intensity that one quantum at a time passes through the apparatus. Each [[photon|photon]] or electron is recorded at a single point on the screen, as a particle would be. The points fall at random, but as thousands accumulate they build up the fringes of [[dslit|double-slit interference]], as a wave would. The electron version, with electrons whose wavelength is given by the [[debrog|de Broglie relation]], was carried out by Pozzi and colleagues and later by Tonomura's group. Closing one slit removes the fringes, and so does any arrangement that records which slit each quantum passed. This is [[duality|wave–particle duality]]: each detection is particle-like, while the probability of where detections occur follows a wave. Neither picture alone describes the experiment.
N M duality | Wave–particle duality | both pictures required | q
d Light and matter both show wave behaviour (interference in the double-slit interference experiment) and particle behaviour (single detections); neither picture alone suffices.
p complementary descriptions; single quanta build up an interference pattern; which-path information destroys interference
g wave-particle duality; double-slit interference experiment
R[thm] duality <- photon, debrog, dslit : one-at-a-time particles build up two-slit fringes | Double slit with single quanta | Detection is particle-like, distribution wave-like.
P Duality limits how sharply the state of a particle can be specified. A wave with a single wave number k extends over all space. A wave confined to a region of width Δx must combine wave numbers with a spread Δk ≥ 1/(2Δx); this is a property of all waves and follows from Fourier analysis. For a matter wave p = ħk by the [[debrog|de Broglie relation]], so multiplying by ħ gives the [[uncert|Heisenberg uncertainty principle]] below, where Δx and Δp are the standard deviations of position and momentum over many identically prepared particles. The limit is not a defect of instruments; it follows from the wave side of [[duality|wave–particle duality]]. Because ħ is so small, it is irrelevant for everyday bodies but decisive inside atoms. Heisenberg, Schrödinger and Dirac turned these ideas into a complete theory in the mid-1920s, the subject of the next chapter.
Q Δx Δp ≥ ħ/2
N M uncert | Uncertainty principle | Δx Δp ≥ ħ/2 | q
d Position and momentum cannot both be sharp: the product of their uncertainties is at least ħ/2.
p a property of waves (Fourier pairs), not of measuring apparatus; limits trajectories at atomic scales
g Heisenberg uncertainty principle
R[thm] uncert <- duality, debrog : a wave localised in Δx has a spread Δk ≥ 1/(2Δx) | Matter waves are Fourier pairs | Multiply by ħ.
X electron microscopy :: an application of short de Broglie wavelengths
P Short de Broglie wavelengths make *electron microscopy* possible. Diffraction limits the detail a microscope can resolve to roughly the wavelength used, as the [[rayl|Rayleigh criterion]] expresses, so visible light cannot resolve structures much smaller than 200 nm. Electrons accelerated through 100 kV have a wavelength of 0.0037 nm, found with the relativistic momentum, and magnetic lenses focus them. In a *transmission electron microscope (TEM)* the beam passes through a very thin specimen and forms an image of it. In a *scanning electron microscope (SEM)* a fine beam is scanned across a surface, and the electrons it knocks out are counted point by point to form an image. Lens aberrations, not the wavelength, set the practical limit, of order 0.1 nm in the best instruments. Ruska built the first electron microscope in the early 1930s.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Chapter 7
§ 3 7.0
P Chapter 6 established [[duality|wave–particle duality]] for light and matter and collected rules, such as [[bohr|Bohr's quantization condition]], that fit particular cases. This chapter presents the theory that replaces those rules. The state of a particle is a wave function whose squared magnitude gives probabilities, and the wave function obeys the Schrödinger equation. Applied to a particle in a box, a harmonic oscillator and a potential barrier, the equation produces quantized energies, a nonzero lowest energy and tunneling through barriers. In each case it also recovers classical mechanics in the limit of large quantum numbers.
§ 3 7.1
P The *two-slit interference* of single electrons shows that the behaviour of a particle is governed by a wave. Quantum mechanics takes that wave as the complete description of the particle's state and calls it the [[psi|wave function]] Ψ(x, t). Its model is the [[debrog|de Broglie wave]] of a free particle, written as a [[trav|traveling wave]] in complex form, Ψ(x, t) = A e^{i(kx − ωt)} = A[cos(kx − ωt) + i sin(kx − ωt)], with p = ħk and E = ħω. In general Ψ is a *complex function*, involving i = √−1. The complex form is needed: a real cosine wave has nodes that move through space, whereas a particle of sharp momentum has no preferred position, and the complex wave has |Ψ|² = |A|² everywhere. Ψ itself is not measured; only quantities built from it are.
N S psi | Wave function | Ψ(x, t) | q
d The quantum state of a particle: a complex function of position and time; its symmetry under x → −x may be even or odd.
m complex square-integrable function on configuration space (element of L²), defined up to a global phase
g wave function; complex function; even function; odd function; anti-symmetric function
R[gen] psi <- debrog, trav : complex matter wave A e^{i(kx − ωt)} | Matter wave as state | The de Broglie wave becomes the state itself.
P Born proposed in 1926 that Ψ determines probabilities. In the *Born interpretation*, the probability of finding the particle between x and x + dx at time t is |Ψ(x, t)|² dx = Ψ^{∗}Ψ dx, so |Ψ|² is a *probability density*, with unit m⁻¹ in one dimension. This [[born|Born rule]] is tested by repeating an experiment on many identically prepared particles and recording the distribution of outcomes, as in the build-up of two-slit fringes. Since the particle is certainly somewhere, the probabilities over all x add to one, which is the *normalization condition* below; a wave function is normalized by choosing its amplitude to satisfy it. Multiplying Ψ by a constant phase factor e^{iα} leaves |Ψ|² unchanged everywhere, so such a global phase has no observable effect.
Q ∫_{−∞}^{∞} |Ψ(x, t)|² dx = 1
N M born | Born rule | P = |Ψ|² dx | q ax
d |Ψ(x, t)|² is the probability density for finding the particle at x; the total probability is one (normalization condition).
p probabilities from |Ψ|²; ∫|Ψ|² dx = 1; global phase unobservable
g probability density; Born interpretation; normalization condition
P A measurement of position gives one definite result, not a spread, and an immediate repeat of the measurement gives the same result. The description must therefore change abruptly: the [[psi|wave function]] that supplied the probabilities through the [[born|Born rule]] is replaced by one concentrated at the outcome. This *state reduction*, or *wave function collapse*, is the [[collapse|measurement postulate]] of quantum mechanics. In the *Copenhagen interpretation*, associated with Bohr and Heisenberg, it is part of the rules, and the wave function encodes what can be predicted about outcomes. Whether collapse is a physical process is still debated. *Schrödinger's cat*, whose survival in a closed box depends on a single radioactive decay, dramatizes the question. A *qubit*, a two-state quantum system used to store information, can be in a superposition of both states until it is measured, and quantum computing depends on this.
N M collapse | Measurement and state reduction | Ψ → outcome state | q ax
d A measurement gives one definite outcome, and the wave function is then replaced by one consistent with it (wave function collapse, Copenhagen interpretation).
p probabilistic outcomes; discontinuous change of state on measurement; interpretation debated
g state reduction; wave function collapse; Copenhagen interpretation
R[law] collapse <- born, psi : after the outcome, Ψ → state with that outcome | Postulate | Repeated measurement gives the same result.
P The [[expval|expectation value]] ⟨x⟩ is the average of many position measurements on identically prepared systems. Each x is weighted by its probability density from the [[born|Born rule]], which gives the first formula below. Other quantities are averaged by letting an operator act on the [[psi|wave function]]: the *position operator* multiplies by x, the *momentum operator* is −iħ ∂/∂x, and the kinetic energy follows from ⟨p²⟩/2m. If Ψ is an *even function* of x, or an *odd function*, also called an *anti-symmetric function*, then |Ψ|² is even and ⟨x⟩ = 0. Bohr's [[corresp|correspondence principle]] requires quantum predictions to agree with classical mechanics in the limit of large quantum numbers. In hydrogen, for example, the frequency of light emitted in a jump from n to n − 1 approaches the classical orbital frequency of the electron as n grows. Each model in this chapter can be checked against this limit.
Q ⟨x⟩ = ∫ Ψ^{∗} x Ψ dx
Q ⟨p⟩ = ∫ Ψ^{∗} (−iħ ∂Ψ/∂x) dx
N O expval | Expectation value | ⟨x⟩ = ∫Ψ* x Ψ dx | q
d Average of many measurements on identically prepared systems; position and momentum act as operators x and −iħ ∂/∂x.
g expectation value; position operator; momentum operator
R[thm] expval <- psi, born : ⟨x⟩ = ∫Ψ* x Ψ dx,  ⟨p⟩ = ∫Ψ*(−iħ ∂/∂x)Ψ dx | Weighted average | Weighted by the probability density.
N M corresp | Correspondence principle | quantum → classical at large n | q ax
d In the limit of large energies (large quantum numbers) quantum predictions agree with classical mechanics.
p limiting principle; links the two theories; guides construction of quantum theories
g correspondence principle

§ 3 7.2
X Heisenberg’s uncertainty principle :: = uncert
P A particle with sharp momentum is described by a single plane wave, which has the same |Ψ|² everywhere. A localized particle is described by a [[wpack|wave packet]]. By the [[superp|superposition principle]], plane waves e^{i(kx − ωt)} with amplitudes A(k), spread over a range of [[kwave|wave numbers]], add to a [[psi|wave function]] that is large only in a region of width Δx. Fourier analysis shows that Δx Δk ≥ ½, with equality for a Gaussian packet: the narrower the packet, the more wave numbers it needs. With p = ħk this is [[uncert|Heisenberg's uncertainty principle]], Δx Δp ≥ ħ/2. An electron confined to Δx = 0.10 nm, the size of an atom, has Δp ≥ 5.3 × 10⁻²⁵ kg·m/s, a velocity spread of at least 5.8 × 10⁵ m/s, so it cannot follow a definite orbit.
Q Ψ(x, t) = ∫ A(k) e^{i(kx − ωt)} dk
N S wpack | Wave packet | ∫ A(k) e^{i(kx − ωt)} dk | q
d A localised wave made by superposing plane waves of nearby wave numbers; the narrower it is in x, the broader in k.
m superposition of plane waves with amplitude A(k); Δx Δk ≥ ½
g wave packet
R[thm] wpack <- psi, superp, kwave : sum of plane waves | Superposition | The free-particle state with finite extent.
R[thm] uncert <- wpack : Δx Δk ≥ ½ and p = ħk | Uncertainty from Fourier analysis | Narrow packets need many wave numbers.
P The same Fourier relation holds between time and angular frequency: a wave train lasting a time Δt contains angular frequencies spread over Δω ≥ 1/(2Δt). For a [[photon|photon]], and for any quantum state, E = ħω, so multiplying by ħ gives the [[uncertE|energy–time uncertainty principle]] below. Here Δt is not the spread of a measured time but the time over which the state changes appreciably, such as the lifetime of an excited atom. A state with lifetime 1.0 × 10⁻⁸ s has an energy spread of at least ħ/(2Δt) = 3.3 × 10⁻⁸ eV, so the photons it emits are not exactly monochromatic; this sets the natural width of spectral lines, and short-lived states give broad lines. Like the [[uncert|position–momentum uncertainty principle]], it is a property of waves, not of instruments.
Q ΔE Δt ≥ ħ/2
N M uncertE | Energy–time uncertainty | ΔE Δt ≥ ħ/2 | q
d A state that lasts only Δt has an energy spread at least ħ/(2Δt); short-lived states have broad lines.
p E = ħω and Δω Δt ≥ ½; sets natural line widths
g energy-time uncertainty principle
R[thm] uncertE <- uncert, photon : Δω Δt ≥ ½ with E = ħω | Time–frequency pair | The same Fourier relation in time.

§ 3 7.3
P The wave function changes in time according to an equation proposed by Schrödinger in 1926. It cannot be derived from classical physics, but it can be built to reproduce the free [[debrog|de Broglie wave]]. For Ψ = A e^{i(kx − ωt)}, the operation iħ ∂/∂t multiplies the [[psi|wave function]] by ħω = E, and −iħ ∂/∂x multiplies it by ħk = p. Writing the [[Emech|mechanical energy]] E = p²/2m + U with these operators acting on Ψ gives *Schrödinger's time-dependent equation*, the [[tdse|Schrödinger equation]] below, for a particle of mass m with potential energy U(x, t). It is linear, so superpositions of solutions are solutions. It is first order in time, so Ψ at one instant fixes Ψ at all later instants. It contains the *imaginary number* i, so its solutions are complex. For real U it keeps a normalized Ψ normalized.
Q iħ ∂Ψ/∂t = −(ħ²/2m) ∂²Ψ/∂x² + UΨ
N M tdse | Schrödinger equation | iħ ∂Ψ/∂t = −(ħ²/2m) ∂²Ψ/∂x² + UΨ | q ax
d The equation of motion of the wave function: given Ψ at one time and the potential energy U, it fixes Ψ at all times.
p linear (superposition); first order in time; complex; preserves normalization
g Schrödinger’s time-dependent equation
R[thm] tdse <- Emech, debrog, psi : E = p²/2m + U with E → iħ∂/∂t, p → −iħ∂/∂x | From the energy of a particle | Written to reproduce E = ħω and p = ħk for free particles.
P When the potential energy depends on x only, the equation has solutions in which space and time separate. Substituting Ψ(x, t) = ψ(x)φ(t) into the [[tdse|time-dependent equation]] and dividing by ψφ leaves a function of t alone on one side and a function of x alone on the other, so both equal one constant, the energy E. The time part is φ = e^{−iEt/ħ} = e^{−iωt}, the *time-modulation factor*, and ψ(x) satisfies *Schrödinger's time-independent equation*, the [[tise|time-independent Schrödinger equation]] below. It is an eigenvalue problem. For a bound particle, solutions that are finite, continuous and normalizable exist only for particular values of E, and these are the allowed energies. For a free particle, with U = 0, ψ = e^{ikx} is a solution for any E = ħ²k²/2m ≥ 0.
Q −(ħ²/2m) d²ψ/dx² + U(x)ψ = Eψ
N M tise | Time-independent Schrödinger equation | −(ħ²/2m)ψ″ + Uψ = Eψ | q
d For a potential that does not change in time, Ψ = ψ(x)e^{−iωt}; ψ satisfies an eigenvalue equation for the energy, and e^{−iωt} is the time-modulation factor.
p separation of variables; eigenvalue problem for E; real potential gives real energies
g Schrödinger’s time-independent equation; time-modulation factor
R[thm] tise <- tdse : Ψ(x, t) = ψ(x) e^{−iEt/ħ} | Separation of variables | Valid when U does not depend on t.

§ 3 7.4
P The simplest bound system, the [[pib|particle in a box]], is a particle of mass m between rigid walls at x = 0 and x = L: an *infinite square well*, with U = 0 inside and U = ∞ outside. The wave function vanishes outside, so the [[bc|boundary conditions]] are ψ(0) = ψ(L) = 0. Inside, the [[tise|time-independent equation]] gives ψ = A sin kx + B cos kx with k² = 2mE/ħ². The condition at x = 0 requires B = 0, and the one at x = L allows only kL = nπ, the [[standw|standing waves]] of a string fixed at both ends; normalization gives A = √(2/L). The *energy quantum number* n = 1, 2, 3, …, also called the *principal quantum number*, labels the *energy levels* below. Since n = 0 would make ψ vanish, the *ground state energy* E₁ is not zero: for an electron in a box 0.50 nm wide, E₁ = 1.50 eV and E₂ = 6.02 eV.
Q ψ_n(x) = √(2/L) sin(nπx/L),   E_n = n²π²ħ²/(2mL²)
N S pib | Particle in a box | E_n = n²π²ħ²/(2mL²) | q
d A particle confined to an infinite square well: ψ_n = √(2/L) sin(nπx/L), standing matter waves; only discrete energy levels with quantum number n ≥ 1 are allowed, so even the ground state energy is nonzero.
m discrete set of states ψ_n, n = 1, 2, 3, …; energies ∝ n²
g infinite square well; ground state energy; energy quantum number; principal quantum number; energy levels
R[thm] pib <- tise, bc, standw : ψ(0) = ψ(L) = 0 ⇒ k = nπ/L | Boundary-value problem | The string's standing-wave condition for a matter wave.
P Each solution of the [[tise|time-independent equation]], multiplied by its time-modulation factor, is a [[stat|stationary state]] Ψ_n = ψ_n exp(−iE_{n}t/ħ). Because this factor has magnitude one, the probability density |Ψ_n|² = |ψ_n|² does not change in time, and neither does any expectation value. In the [[pib|particle in a box]] these are *standing wave states*. A state that changes in time is a superposition of stationary states with different energies. For large n, the density (2/L) sin²(nπx/L) has n closely spaced peaks, and its average over any small interval approaches the uniform value 1/L. A classical particle bouncing between the walls at constant speed spends equal times in equal intervals, so its [[xt|motion on a line]] gives exactly that uniform density. The relative spacing of the levels, (E_{n+1} − E_n)/E_n = (2n + 1)/n², also goes to zero, and the energy appears continuous, as the [[corresp|correspondence principle]] requires.
N S stat | Stationary state | |Ψ|² constant in time | q
d A solution with one definite energy; its probability density does not change, so it is a standing wave state.
m Ψ_n = ψ_n(x) e^{−iE_nt/ħ}
g stationary state; standing wave state
R[thm] stat <- tise, pib : Ψ_n = ψ_n e^{−iE_nt/ħ} ⇒ |Ψ_n|² = |ψ_n|² | Phase factor drops out | Energy eigenstates do not evolve observably.
R[lim] xt <- pib, corresp : n → ∞: |ψ_n|² averages to the uniform classical density | Classical limit in a box | A ball bouncing between walls spends equal time everywhere.

§ 3 7.5
P Near the bottom of almost any potential well the potential energy is close to quadratic, so the quantum oscillator describes vibrations of molecules and of atoms in solids. Putting the potential energy U = ½mω²x² of the classical [[sho|simple harmonic oscillator]] into the [[tise|time-independent equation]] gives the [[qho|quantum harmonic oscillator]]. Its normalizable solutions are ψ_n = N_n e^{−β²x²/2} H_n(βx), with β = √(mω/ħ), a normalization constant N_n, and a *Hermite polynomial* H_n of degree n. The energies are equally spaced by ħω, the spacing of [[planck|Planck's hypothesis]], but the lowest one is the zero-point energy ħω/2, not zero: a particle at rest at the bottom would have Δx = 0 and Δp = 0, which the [[uncert|uncertainty principle]] forbids. The wave functions also extend beyond the classical turning points, into regions where E < U; in the ground state the particle is found there with probability about 16 %.
Q E_n = (n + ½)ħω,   n = 0, 1, 2, …
N S qho | Quantum harmonic oscillator | E_n = (n + ½)ħω | q
d The Schrödinger equation with U = ½mω²x²: equally spaced levels, a zero-point energy ħω/2, and wave functions built from Hermite polynomials.
m states ψ_n = N_n e^{−β²x²/2} H_n(βx), n = 0, 1, 2, …
g Hermite polynomial
R[thm] qho <- tise, sho : −(ħ²/2m)ψ″ + ½mω²x²ψ = Eψ | Oscillator potential | The classical oscillator's potential energy in the quantum equation.
P The classical and quantum oscillators differ most in their lowest states. A classical particle in [[shm|harmonic oscillation]] with amplitude A moves fastest at the centre and slowest near the turning points ±A, so it is most likely to be found near the ends; its probability density is 1/(π√(A² − x²)) for |x| < A. The quantum ground state instead has its greatest density at the centre. As n increases, |ψ_n|² of the [[qho|quantum oscillator]] develops n + 1 peaks, the largest near the classical turning points, and its average over neighbouring peaks follows the classical curve. Meanwhile ħω becomes a small fraction of E_n, so the steps in energy are no longer noticeable. Both trends illustrate the [[corresp|correspondence principle]]. A mass on a spring with f = 1 Hz and energy 1 J has n ≈ 1.5 × 10³³.
R[lim] shm <- qho, corresp : large n: |ψ_n|² peaks near the classical turning points | Classical limit of the oscillator | Quantum and classical probability densities agree on average.

§ 3 7.6
P A *potential barrier* is a region where the potential energy exceeds the energy of an approaching particle. Take U = U₀ for 0 ≤ x ≤ L, U = 0 elsewhere, and a particle in a [[stat|stationary state]] of energy E < U₀. Inside the barrier the [[tise|time-independent equation]] gives ψ″ = β²ψ with β² = 2m(U₀ − E)/ħ², so ψ is a combination of e^{−βx} and e^{βx}, not an oscillation. Matching ψ and ψ′ at both edges to the incident, reflected and transmitted waves leaves a transmitted wave of nonzero amplitude: the particle crosses by *quantum tunneling*. The ratio |ψ_{tra}|²/|ψ_{in}|² is the *transmission probability*, or [[Ttun|tunneling probability]] T; for βL ≫ 1 it is approximately the expression below. For an electron with E = 3.0 eV and U₀ = 4.0 eV, β = 5.12 × 10⁹ m⁻¹; a 1.0 nm barrier gives T ≈ 1.1 × 10⁻⁴, and a 2.0 nm barrier about 4 × 10⁻⁹.
Q T ≈ 16(E/U₀)(1 − E/U₀) e^{−2βL}
N O Ttun | Tunneling probability | T ≈ 16(E/U₀)(1 − E/U₀) e^{−2βL} | q
d A particle can cross a potential barrier higher than its energy; the transmission probability falls exponentially with barrier width and with √(U₀ − E).
g quantum tunneling; potential barrier; transmission probability; tunneling probability
R[thm] Ttun <- tise, stat : ψ decays as e^{−βx} inside, β² = 2m(U₀ − E)/ħ² | Evanescent wave in the barrier | Matching ψ and ψ′ at both edges.
P Classical mechanics forbids this. On a [[pediag|potential-energy diagram]], a particle with E < U₀ reaches a turning point at the edge of the barrier, where its kinetic energy E − U falls to zero; inside, the kinetic energy would be negative. It is reflected with certainty, T = 0, however thin the barrier. Observation disagrees. Hund used tunneling to explain features of molecular spectra, and in 1928 Gamow explained how heavy nuclei emit α particles of a few MeV through a Coulomb barrier several times higher; the exponential sensitivity of T accounts for α-decay half-lives that range from below a microsecond to billions of years. Strong electric fields pull electrons out of cold metals through the surface barrier, which is *field emission*. In each case the [[Ttun|tunneling probability]] of the wave description replaces the classical certainty of reflection.
R[fail] Ttun <- pediag : E < U₀ ⇒ turning point, T = 0 | Classical barrier is impenetrable | Observed in α decay, field emission and the scanning tunneling microscope.
X field emission; nanotechnology; quantum dot; resonant tunneling; resonant-tunneling diode; scanning tunneling microscope (STM); tunnel diode :: devices and phenomena that apply tunneling
P Tunneling is the working principle of several devices. In the *scanning tunneling microscope (STM)*, a sharp metal tip is held a fraction of a nanometre above a conducting surface, and a small voltage drives a tunneling current across the gap. The current changes by roughly a factor of ten for each 0.1 nm change in the gap, so scanning the tip while keeping the current constant maps the surface with atomic resolution. A *tunnel diode* passes current through a very thin junction by tunneling. In *resonant tunneling*, two thin barriers enclose a narrow well whose levels act like those of a box, and electrons whose energy matches a level cross both barriers with high probability; the *resonant-tunneling diode* uses this to switch very fast. A *quantum dot*, a tiny region that confines electrons in all three directions, has discrete levels of the same kind. Such devices belong to *nanotechnology*.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Chapter 8
§ 3 8.0
P [[bohr|Bohr's model]] gave the energy levels of hydrogen but not a consistent theory of atoms. This chapter applies the [[tdse|Schrödinger equation]] of Chapter 7 to atoms. For hydrogen it yields the energy levels, the shapes of the electron's probability distributions and the quantization of angular momentum. Magnetic effects then reveal the electron's orbital magnetic moment and its intrinsic spin. Together with the exclusion principle, the same quantum numbers explain the periodic table, and transitions between atomic levels account for spectra, X-rays and lasers.
§ 3 8.1
P In the hydrogen atom the electron moves in the potential energy U(r) = −ke²/r of the proton, given by [[coul|Coulomb's law]]. The [[tise|time-independent Schrödinger equation]] in three dimensions separates in spherical coordinates, and its normalizable solutions ψ_{nlm}(r, θ, φ), the *atomic orbitals*, are labelled by three integers: the *principal quantum number* n = 1, 2, 3, …, the *angular momentum orbital quantum number* l = 0, 1, …, n − 1, and the *angular momentum projection quantum number* m = −l, …, l. The energies depend on n alone and equal Bohr's [[bohrlev|hydrogen energy levels]], E_n = −13.6 eV/n², now obtained without postulated orbits. The [[hatom|hydrogen atom]] of quantum mechanics gives probabilities, not paths. For states that do not depend on angle, the *radial probability density function* P(r) = 4πr²|ψ|² is the probability per unit distance of finding the electron at radius r. For the normalized ground state below, P(r) peaks at r = a₀, the Bohr radius.
Q ψ₁₀₀ = e^{−r/a₀} / √(πa₀³)
N S hatom | Hydrogen atom | ψ_nlm, E_n = −13.6 eV/n² | q
d Solutions of the Schrödinger equation for the Coulomb potential: atomic orbitals labelled by n, l (0 … n − 1) and m (−l … l). Energies depend only on n and agree with Bohr's; electrons have no definite orbits.
m eigenfunctions ψ_nlm(r, θ, φ); n ≥ 1, 0 ≤ l < n, |m| ≤ l; radial probability density P(r) = r²|R_{nl}(r)|², equal to 4πr²|ψ|² for l = 0
g principal quantum number (n); angular momentum orbital quantum number (l); angular momentum projection quantum number (m); radial probability density function; atomic orbital
R[thm] hatom <- tise, coul : U(r) = −ke²/r in three dimensions | Coulomb potential | Separable in spherical coordinates.
R[thm] bohrlev <- hatom : E_n = −13.6 eV/n² | Bohr's energies recovered | Without Bohr's orbits.
P The angular part of ψ_{nlm} fixes the orbital [[Lang|angular momentum]] L⃗ = r⃗ × p⃗. Applying the operator for L⃗ to the [[hatom|hydrogen wave functions]] shows that its magnitude and its component along one chosen axis, called z, take only the discrete values below; this is [[Lq|quantized angular momentum]]. In *spectroscopic notation* l = 0, 1, 2, 3 are written s, p, d, f, so the *ground state* is 1s, with L = 0 rather than the ħ of Bohr's first orbit. Because L_z = mħ is smaller than L for every l ≥ 1, L⃗ never points exactly along z: for l = 2 and m = 2, cos θ = 2/√6 and the angle to the axis is 35.3°. The components L_x and L_y then remain uncertain. For each n there are n values of l and 2l + 1 values of m, so each energy level holds n² orbitals.
Q L = √(l(l + 1)) ħ,   L_z = mħ
N O Lq | Quantized angular momentum | L = √(l(l+1)) ħ,  L_z = mħ | q
d Magnitude and one component of orbital angular momentum take only discrete values.
R[thm] Lq <- hatom, Lang : L = √(l(l+1))ħ, L_z = mħ | Angular part of the solution | Operators for r⃗ × p⃗ on ψ_nlm.

§ 3 8.2
P A charge moving on a closed path forms a current loop and so has a [[mu|magnetic dipole moment]]. Classically, an electron on a circle of radius r at speed v passes each point once per period 2πr/v, which is a [[curr|current]] I = ev/(2πr); with area A = πr² the moment is μ = IA = evr/2. Since L = m_e v r, μ = (e/2m_e)L, and because the electron is negative the moment points opposite to L⃗. The relation carries over to quantum mechanics with the [[Lq|quantized angular momentum]]: the [[muorb|orbital magnetic dipole moment]] has z-component μ_z = −mμ_B, where m is here called the *magnetic orbital quantum number* and μ_B = eħ/(2m_e) = 9.27 × 10⁻²⁴ J/T = 5.79 × 10⁻⁵ eV/T is the *Bohr magneton*.
Q μ⃗ = −(e/2m_e) L⃗
N O muorb | Orbital magnetic moment | μ⃗ = −(e/2m_e) L⃗ | q
d The orbiting electron is a current loop; its moment comes in units of the Bohr magneton μ_B = eħ/2m_e.
g orbital magnetic dipole moment; Bohr magneton; magnetic orbital quantum number
R[thm] muorb <- mu, Lq, curr : μ = IA with I = ev/2πr, L = m_e v r | Current loop of an orbit | Moment proportional to angular momentum.
P In an external magnetic field B⃗ along z, a magnetic dipole has [[U|potential energy]] U = −μ⃗·B⃗ = −μ_z B. With the [[muorb|orbital moment]], each level therefore shifts by U = mμ_B B. A level with orbital quantum number l splits into 2l + 1 equally spaced sublevels, μ_B B apart: 5.79 × 10⁻⁵ eV for a [[Bval|field strength]] of 1.00 T. Spectral lines from transitions between such levels split into several closely spaced components. This is the [[zeeman|Zeeman effect]]. It measures magnetic fields where no probe can go; a *magnetogram* of the Sun maps the field across its surface from the Zeeman splitting of lines in sunlight. The electron's spin, treated next, makes most observed splittings more complicated than this simple pattern.
Q U = mμ_B B
N O zeeman | Zeeman effect | ΔE = m μ_B B | q
d In a magnetic field, levels with different m split, and spectral lines split into several components.
g Zeeman effect
R[thm] zeeman <- muorb, Bval, U : U = −μ⃗·B⃗ = m μ_B B | Dipole energy in a field | 2l + 1 equally spaced sublevels.
X magnetogram :: an application: maps of the Sun's field from Zeeman splitting

§ 3 8.3
P In 1922 Stern and Gerlach sent a beam of silver atoms through a nonuniform [[Bfield|magnetic field]]. An atom with [[mu|magnetic moment]] component μ_z feels a force μ_z ∂B_z/∂z, so the beam spreads according to the values of μ_z. A classical moment with random orientation would give a continuous smear, and an orbital moment would give 2l + 1 beams, an odd number; the outer electron of silver, moreover, has l = 0. In this *Stern–Gerlach experiment* the beam split into two. The electron must therefore carry an intrinsic [[Lang|angular momentum]], [[spin|electron spin]], with *spin quantum number* s = ½, magnitude S = √(s(s + 1)) ħ = (√3/2)ħ, and component S_z = m_s ħ with *spin projection quantum number* m_s = ±½. Its magnetic moment is μ⃗_s = −(e/m_e)S⃗, twice the orbital value per unit angular momentum, so μ_z = ∓μ_B. An electron in hydrogen is thus described by five quantum numbers: n, l, m, s and m_s.
N O spin | Electron spin | S = √(s(s+1)) ħ,  S_z = m_s ħ | q
d Intrinsic angular momentum of the electron, s = ½ with m_s = ±½, shown by the two beams of the Stern–Gerlach experiment; it carries a magnetic moment μ⃗_s ≈ −(e/m_e)S⃗, antiparallel to the spin because the electron's charge is negative.
g spin quantum number (s); spin projection quantum number (ms)
R[meas] spin <- mu, Bfield, Lang : atomic beam splits in two in a nonuniform field | Stern–Gerlach | Only two orientations, not 2l + 1.
P Spin also couples to fields inside the atom. In the electron's rest frame the proton circles the electron, a current loop like the one behind the [[muorb|orbital magnetic moment]], and produces a magnetic field proportional to the [[Lq|orbital angular momentum]]. The energy of the [[spin|spin]] magnetic moment in this field is U = −μ⃗_s·B⃗ ∝ S⃗·L⃗, the [[soc|spin-orbit coupling]]. It splits each level with l ≥ 1 into two, according to whether S⃗ is roughly parallel or antiparallel to L⃗, and so produces the *fine structure* of spectral lines; the yellow sodium line, for example, is a pair at 589.0 nm and 589.6 nm. The electron's moment also interacts with the magnetic moment of the nucleus, hundreds of times smaller, giving the *hyperfine structure*. In the ground state of hydrogen, *spin-flip transitions* between the two hyperfine levels emit photons of 5.9 × 10⁻⁶ eV, the 21 cm line that radio astronomers use to map hydrogen in space.
N M soc | Spin–orbit and hyperfine coupling | U ∝ S⃗·L⃗ | q
d The electron's spin moment interacts with the field of its orbital motion (fine structure) and with the nuclear spin (hyperfine structure, spin-flip transitions such as hydrogen's 21-cm line).
p splits levels of equal n and l; small compared with the Coulomb energy; spin-flip emits a photon
g spin-orbit coupling; fine structure; hyperfine structure; spin-flip transitions
R[thm] soc <- spin, Lq, muorb : U = −μ⃗_s·B⃗_orbit ∝ S⃗·L⃗ | Magnetic energy of spin | Internal magnetic field of the orbital motion.

§ 3 8.4
P In an atom with many electrons, each electron moves in the field of the nucleus and of the other electrons, and its states can still be labelled by n, l, m and m_s. How the electrons occupy these states is fixed by the [[pauli|Pauli exclusion principle]], stated in 1925: no two electrons in an atom can have the same set of quantum numbers (n, l, m, m_s). The principle holds for all particles of half-integer spin. States with the same n form a *shell*, and states with the same n and l form a *subshell*. A subshell has 2l + 1 values of m and two values of m_s, so it holds at most 4l + 2 electrons: 2 in an s subshell, 6 in p, 10 in d and 14 in f. A shell holds 2n². Without the principle, all electrons would settle into the 1s state and every atom would behave alike chemically.
Q N = 4l + 2
N M pauli | Pauli exclusion principle | one electron per state | q ax
d No two electrons in an atom can have the same set of quantum numbers (n, l, m, m_s).
p applies to electrons (fermions); subshell holds at most 4l + 2 electrons; builds the periodic table
g Pauli’s exclusion principle
P In its ground state an atom's Z electrons occupy the lowest-energy states available, one per state. The states carry the labels of the [[hatom|hydrogen atom]] plus [[spin|spin]], and the [[pauli|exclusion principle]] limits each subshell, so the ground state is given by an [[econf|electron configuration]]: sodium (Z = 11) is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹. Inner electrons screen the nuclear charge, and s electrons penetrate closest to the nucleus, so the energy depends on l as well as n. Subshells fill in the order 1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p, 4s, 3d, 4p, …; iron (Z = 26) is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s² 3d⁶. The *valence electrons* in the outermost shell set the chemistry. Elements with the same valence configuration form a *chemical group* of the *periodic table*, such as the alkali metals with one s electron, and the *transition metals* are those filling a d subshell. Transfer or sharing of valence electrons produces ionic and covalent bonds.
N S econf | Electron configuration | 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ … | q
d Electrons fill subshells in order of energy, each with at most 4l + 2; the outermost (valence) electrons fix chemical groups and the transition metals.
m occupation numbers of subshells (n, l) consistent with the exclusion principle
g electron configuration; valence electron; chemical group; transition metal
R[thm] econf <- hatom, spin, pauli : fill states (n, l, m, m_s) once each, lowest energy first | Building up the periodic table | Shells and subshells from the hydrogen labels.
X ionic bond; covalent bond :: = bonds

§ 3 8.5
P An atom emits or absorbs a [[photon|photon]] when an electron moves between levels, with hf equal to the energy difference. Not every pair of levels is connected. The photon carries one unit ħ of angular momentum, so by [[Lcons|conservation of angular momentum]] the orbital quantum number of the atom must change by one. These are the [[selrule|selection rules]] for electric dipole transitions, Δl = ±1 and Δm = 0, ±1, stated in terms of the [[Lq|quantized angular momentum]]. In hydrogen the transition 2p → 1s is allowed and gives the Lyman α line at 121.6 nm, while 2s → 1s is forbidden, so the 2s state lives far longer. An atom excited to a high level often returns in several smaller steps. In *fluorescence*, ultraviolet light absorbed by a material is re-emitted this way as visible light of lower frequency.
Q Δl = ±1
N M selrule | Selection rules | Δl = ±1 | q
d Atomic transitions that emit or absorb a photon change l by one, because the photon carries one unit of angular momentum.
p Δl = ±1 for electric dipole transitions; other transitions strongly suppressed
g selection rules
R[thm] selrule <- photon, Lq, Lcons : photon carries angular momentum ħ | Angular momentum conservation | Atom plus photon conserve angular momentum.
P X-rays come from the inner electrons of heavy atoms. In an X-ray tube, electrons accelerated through a voltage V strike a metal target, and their deceleration produces *braking radiation*, a continuous spectrum that ends at λ = hc/(eV). Sharp characteristic lines, the [[xray|atomic X-rays]], stand out above it. They appear when a 1s electron of a target atom is knocked out and an electron from the n = 2 shell falls into the vacancy, emitting a Kα [[photon|photon]]. In the [[econf|electron configuration]] one 1s electron remains and screens one unit of nuclear charge, so the falling electron sees about (Z − 1)e. The [[bohrlev|hydrogen-like energy levels]] then give the frequency below, which is *Moseley's law*: √f is proportional to Z − 1. For copper (Z = 29) it gives 8.0 keV, close to the measured line. A *Moseley plot* of √f against Z is a straight line; Moseley used it to order the elements by atomic number.
Q hf ≈ (Z − 1)² E₀ (1 − 1/4)
N O xray | Atomic X-rays | √f ∝ (Z − 1) | q
d Heavy atoms emit characteristic X-ray lines when an inner vacancy is filled, with frequencies following Moseley's law; a continuous braking-radiation spectrum accompanies them.
g braking radiation; Moseley’s law; Moseley plot
R[thm] xray <- econf, bohrlev, photon : hf = (Z − 1)²E₀(1 − 1/4) for the Kα line | Hydrogen-like inner electron | The remaining 1s electron screens one unit of nuclear charge.
X fluorescence :: re-emission of light at lower frequency after excitation, described qualitatively

§ 3 8.6
P An atom in an excited level can emit a photon spontaneously, at a random time and in a random direction. Einstein showed in 1917 that a [[photon|photon]] whose energy matches the gap between two [[bohrlev|energy levels]] can also trigger the emission. In this *stimulated emission* the new photon has the same frequency, phase, polarization and direction as the one that triggered it. The same photon is equally likely to be absorbed by an atom in the lower level, so light is amplified only when the upper level holds more atoms than the lower one. In thermal equilibrium the opposite holds; for a gap of 2.0 eV at 300 K the ratio is about 2.5 × 10⁻³⁴. A *population inversion* is created by pumping atoms to a higher level, from which they drop quickly into a long-lived *metastable state*. Mirrors send the light back and forth through the medium. The output of such a [[laser|laser]] is intense, [[coh|coherent]] and highly *monochromatic*.
N M laser | Laser | stimulated emission, population inversion | q
d An incoming photon stimulates an excited atom to emit an identical photon; with more atoms in an excited metastable state than below it (population inversion) the light is amplified into coherent, monochromatic light.
p stimulated photon has same frequency, phase, direction; needs population inversion; output coherent and monochromatic
g laser; stimulated emission; population inversion; metastable state; coherent light; monochromatic
R[thm] laser <- bohrlev, photon, coh : stimulated emission outpaces absorption when N_upper > N_lower | Light amplification | Two-level transitions with an inverted population.
P Lasers are used to read optical discs. A CD, DVD or *Blu-Ray player* focuses a [[laser|laser]] beam to a small spot on a spiral track of pits and flat regions and detects the reflected light. The pits are about a quarter wavelength deep in the plastic of the disc, so light reflected from a pit bottom and from the surrounding surface differs in path by half a wavelength and interferes destructively; the edges of pits therefore show up as changes in reflected intensity, which encode the data. This works because laser light is [[coh|coherent]]. The smallest spot is limited by diffraction, as in the [[rayl|Rayleigh criterion]], and scales with the wavelength. CD players use 780 nm infrared light, DVD players 650 nm red light and Blu-ray players 405 nm violet light, so the shortest wavelength packs the most data onto a disc of the same size.
